Composite invariant (step-12 brief / spec §4.5-§4.6): washi grain is procedural
in SCREEN space and must stay pixel-locked while the field scrolls; if the
grain flips or scrolls, a flip landed in the wrong pass.

Check 1 — same run, ROLL_H (layout 3), --drop-test 3 --dip-burst 2:
two burst prints (dips at t=2.0 and t=2.2; the second sheet is fresh after the
first dip's reset):
  ink-free left margin (cols 0-600): max|d| = 0 (8-bit)
  -> washi grain identical between prints: PIXEL-LOCKED, and deterministic
     across paper dips.

Check 2 — two runs of the same scripted performance (--layout 3 --drop-test 3,
scroll = 120 BPM x roll_speed 0.0625 = 0.125 canvas/s), dipped at t=2.0 (A)
vs t=2.2 (B):
  ink-free left margin (cols 0-560): max|d| = 0  -> grain pixel-locked ACROSS
     runs (pure screen-space procedural, no field dependence).
  ink bounding box (R < 150):
     A: cols 736..1184, rows 135..584, centroid x = 962.3
     B: cols 769..1217, rows 135..584, centroid x = 995.3
  -> ink translated +33 px in 0.2 s (expected 0.2 s x 0.125 canvas/s x 1280 px
     = 32 px; live-clock runs, so +/- a few px), rows identical (pure +x
     translation, correct scroll direction for ROLL_H).

Ink rides the scroll, paper does not, and nothing is mirrored: the GL
orientation handling sits in exactly the right passes.
